@Rosuav sorry about missing out the BytesIO import, original post now fixed.
I started out trying to use ast but hit problems of going line-by-line with an if as it was a single line and needed a following indented line to avoid an error.  Then there was the problem of multi-line statements which was a separate can-of-worms to contend with.
I was hoping that perhaps someone might be able to see the bug and fix this #comments issue in CPython’s tokenizer.

@mfile_bay That’s a shame I was just reading that.
@Rosuav but comments don’t care about indentation..  Yep but just because the docs seem to suggest this ‘incorrect’ behaviour is allowed wouldn’t it be better if the indentation of comments was actually corrected in the first place?  It is only the order the indent / dedent tokens are getting written after a #comments line which cause this, surely this can’t be too difficult to fix can it.
I am using these comments as part of the compiler directives so in this case the faulty indentation may actually be important.

@aroberge I can see how that would allow me to change the token string but would it also allow me to both add tokens and remove tokens without leaving orphaned newlines?
Edit:
Replacement works but I can’t see how to delete tokens at the moment.
